{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfqr3pNsoaJXyBLJ4wut1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This sitting ended mixed: all three amended rows are blessed and re-anchored, and I approved two of the four new rows. I returned the other two on one untested rule. The verdict is `VERDICT: MEANING rows=3` and `OUTCOME: RETURN rows=4`. All three commits carry the `WI: WI-865` trailer, and the pre-commit hooks passed.\n\n**Amendment: SR-233, LLR-311 and TC-332 all changed meaning, and I blessed all three.** The old text fixed the set of finding-judging briefs at the reviewer brief alone. The new text also binds the new dispute brief to cite §6.\n- The shipped `adjudicate-dispute.template.md` cites `§6 \"Review threat model\"` and repeats none of its four examples.\n- `FINDING_JUDGING_BRIEFS` now lists both briefs, and the test passes.\n\n**First approval:**\n- **SR-234 is approved.** Every acceptance clause has a test: the findings composed word for word, the malformed-input refusals, the verdict grammar's accept and refuse cases, a dispute verdict authorizing no approval act, and the entry point refusing an empty request before launch.\n- **LLR-315 is approved.** Every clause of the brief assembly has a test.\n- **LLR-314 and TC-334 are returned.** LLR-314 requires each `[[finding]]` table to carry exactly its four keys. When I weakened that check in my working tree to allow extra keys, all 33 dispute tests still passed; I then restored the file. A table with a missing key isn't tested either; it would surface as a `KeyError` rather than a refusal. Separately, LLR-314 says the texts stay verbatim \"apart from TOML delimiter newlines\", but the code strips every leading and trailing newline.\n\n**Commits:**\n- `b48f96cf` — the verdict, plus one test-and-text follow-up in WI-865's spec `## Dispositions`; the intake parser reads it cleanly.\n- `4a7f2931` — re-attests SR-233, LLR-311 and TC-332 (act 68).\n- `6c77e7fa` — flips only SR-234 and LLR-315 to `Approved` and snapshots the system and design registries (act 69, `approved = [\"LLR-315\", \"SR-234\"]`). I dropped the test-case token because TC-334, its only row, was returned. LLR-314 and TC-334 stay `Drafted`, and both copies match their live registries.\n\n**For the coordinator:** this lane used `acts.toml` seq 68 and 69. The WI-860 and WI-866 lanes are also writing that ledger, so expect sequence numbers to collide again at merge. Also, the RE-ATTEST act on SR-233, LLR-311 and TC-332 here only matches WI-860's approval if WI-860 has landed on trunk before this lane merges.\n\nI left the `.requested` marker alone."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":903,"cache_read_input_tokens":353758,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":903},"output_tokens":779,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8cf33a7-067d-4b49-9e30-523229883c86","uuid":"e2ae9076-75d1-4b4c-ae15-65c80fd4ba29","timestamp":"2026-10-09T02:07:17.131Z","request_id":"req_011Cfqr3omus9kSZ4eoUvFpd"}
